{"reference":"Acts 19","book":"Acts","chapter":19,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The opening disciples","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Acts 19:1-7 presents baptism, the laying on of hands, reception of the Holy Spirit, tongues, and prophecy in this particular order. Other prominent scenes in Acts vary which of these elements appear and how they are ordered. The episode therefore should not be turned into a required sequence for every believer, or into a test in which the absence of one sign proves exclusion. The undisputed letters address contemporary assembly concerns, while Acts is a later narrative with its own sequence, wording, and aims.","verse_refs":["Acts 19:1-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_019_6911e2597b54d1fe80d70536"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Teaching, healing, and ritual practice","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Paul's work moves from synagogue reasoning to daily discussion in the school of Tyrannus, followed by a summary that people throughout Asia heard the word. This describes varied venues and sustained teaching, but it does not establish the precise ownership of the school or turn the regional summary into a numerical count. The later scenes sharply distinguish healings associated with Paul from itinerant exorcists who invoke Jesus' name as a formula and fail. The public confessions and voluntary burning of costly books belong to that contrast. Written formulas and powerful names circulated in ritual practice, but the books in Acts are not thereby identified with a particular Ephesian collection, nor is magic made an ethnic characteristic.","verse_refs":["Acts 19:8-20","Acts 19:1-10","Acts 19:11-20","Acts 19:23-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_019_f0d3b03bcfd65225f2b5f770","reader_path_acts_019_a9a75868f33c938b788fa866"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"The Artemis protest","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Demetrius's speech joins artisan livelihood, craft reputation, Artemis's honor, and the city's public standing. The long-lived sanctuary and its civic reach help explain why the goddess's honor could be presented as a citywide concern rather than merely a private preference. At the same time, this context does not prove a formal guild or monopoly, identify Demetrius's workshop, or verify every detail of the crowd scene.","verse_refs":["Acts 19:23-27","Acts 19:23-41","Acts 19:31-41"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_019_4173df76ea60de62fe8492e7"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"The theater and civic order","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The theater and civic inscriptions illuminate the setting of the crowd and the chapter's references to Asiarchs, the town clerk, courts, proconsuls, a regular assembly, and liability for a riot. They make the civic vocabulary intelligible, but they do not demonstrate the particular crowd, speech, route, or procedure narrated here. Acts also places this episode within selected civic-political scenes, including earlier accusations at Thessalonica; that narrative pattern does not show that Roman institutions were consistently fair.","verse_refs":["Acts 19:28-41","Acts 19:21-41"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_019_4919a3f0a6fff292b9dc53ff","reader_path_acts_019_694a1d64f76e5e24660cdf3a","reader_path_acts_019_9606512c5cc51d00f86014e0"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Acts as a historical narrative","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Historical evaluation of Acts is best done claim by claim, comparing particular details with independent evidence rather than assigning the whole work blanket reliability or unreliability. For reconstructing chronology, the direct letters should be considered first; Acts 13-20 is a later narrative witness whose sequencing, speeches, travel compression, and emphases should not silently replace the letters' own plans and silences.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_019_4af426baa9965ab8aece12b9","reader_path_acts_019_f05e08b2d8c4031301d26dda"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}